course: dictionary
lesson: 12380
title: Crystallised: three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. Crystallised is a good example. It can mean something has turned into crystals, something has become clear and fixed, or food is coated in sugar.
explain: «Salt crystallised on the edge of the pan as the water dried up.» The water left and a solid formed, so crystallised here means turned into crystals. Look for the clues: the water dried up, and hard pieces were left behind.
explain: «After weeks of thinking, her plan finally crystallised.» A plan cannot turn into a crystal, so the clue is different. The plan became clear and firm, so crystallised here means became clear and fixed. Swap in your meaning to check: «her plan finally became clear and fixed» still makes sense.
explain: «The crystallised ginger tasted sweet and sticky.» The clues sweet and sticky tell you the ginger is coated in sugar. Here crystallised is an adjective, describing the ginger.
fact: Crystallised is the past form of the verb crystallise, so it tells you that something has already happened.
fact: «Crystallised» can mean turned into crystals, as when salt or sugar forms solid pieces as water dries up.
fact: «Crystallised» can also mean clear and fixed, as when an idea or plan takes a definite shape.
fact: As an adjective, «crystallised» describes food coated or preserved in sugar, such as «crystallised fruit» or «crystallised ginger».
fact: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «The salt crystallised as the water evaporated.» What does crystallised mean here? | Melted into a liquid | *Turned into crystals | Became sad and quiet | Coated in sugar
exercise: «Her idea crystallised into a clear plan.» What does crystallised mean here? | Went cold and hard like ice | *Became clear and fixed | Turned into crystals | Got lost in the rain
exercise: What does crystallised mean in «crystallised ginger»? | Full of water | *Coated in sugar so it sparkles | Turned into salt | Frozen solid like ice
test: «The scientist watched the sugar crystallise as the syrup cooled.» What does crystallise mean here? | *Form solid crystal pieces as the syrup cools | Turn bright red | Float away into the air | Melt into a puddle
test: «After the meeting, the team's goal finally crystallised.» What does crystallised mean here? | *Became clear and firm | Was broken into pieces | Was painted blue | Was forgotten by everyone
exam: «Crystallised fruit is sweet because it is coated in sugar.» What does crystallised describe? | Salt that has dried out in the sun | A plan that has become clear | *Fruit that is coated or preserved in sugar | A cold, hard puddle
